    </content>
    <glossary>
    <definition id="def-00001"><term>crystallized intelligence</term><meaning>existing knowledge that individuals have developed during their life through education and experience</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00002"><term>fluid intelligence</term><meaning>ability to use logic and to solve problems in new ways</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00003"><term>Flynn effect</term><meaning>effect that describes the significant increases of scores on intelligence tests over time</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00004"><term>general intelligence factor (g)</term><meaning>general cognitive factor that underlies multiple cognitive skills</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00005"><term>giftedness</term><meaning>variation in intelligence marked by cognitive flexibility, cognitive performance, specific ability areas, and an IQ above 130</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00006"><term>intellectual disability</term><meaning>disability that consists of limits in intellectual functioning, often indicated by an IQ score less than 70 and challenges in adaptive functioning</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00007"><term>intelligence</term><meaning>description of an individual’s ability to adapt to the world around them</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00008"><term>intelligence quotient (IQ)</term><meaning>score used to quantify human intelligence</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00009"><term>mental age</term><meaning>age at which a person is performing based on an intelligence test</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00010"><term>triarchic theory of intelligence</term><meaning>theory of intelligence that proposes that there are three types of intelligence: analytical, creative and practical</meaning></definition>
    </glossary>
</document>